<div class='content-container--static-page'>
  <!--Content container to hold Sinai static page navigation-->
  <div class='content-container--sinai-static-page'>
    <%= render 'sinai_secondary_nav' %>
    <!--Start static content-->
    <div class="sinai-static-page-inner-container">
      <div class='static-page__title-row'>
        <h1 class='static-page__title--sinai'>About the Project</h1>
      </div>

      <div class='static-page__row'>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
            The Sinai Manuscripts Digital Library (SMDL) is a publication of St. Catherine’s Monastery of the Sinai, Egypt, in collaboration with the UCLA Library and the Early Manuscripts Electronic Library (EMEL).  It brings together several ongoing initiatives to image, describe, and provide online access to the Monastery’s unparalleled manuscript collection:
        </p>

        <ul class='static-page__list static-page__list--sinai'>
          <li><strong><%= link_to 'Sinai Library Digitization Project', sinai_library_digitization_project_path %></strong> — Imaging and publication of the Monastery’s manuscripts in two phases; Phase One (2018–2022) covered the Arabic and Syriac manuscripts, and Phase Two will cover the Monastery’s collections in Greek, Georgian, Slavonic, and other languages.</li>
          <li><strong><%= link_to 'Sinai Palimpsests Project', sinai_palimpsests_project_path %></strong> (2011–2016) — Multispectral imaging and scholarly description of the undertext objects preserved in a subset of the Monastery’s palimpsested manuscripts.</li>
          <li><strong><%= link_to 'Syriac Parchment Descriptions Project', syriac_parchment_descriptions_project_path %></strong> (2022–2025) — NEH-funded text identification and description of the Monastery’s Syriac manuscripts on parchment.</li>
          <li><strong><%= link_to 'Sinai Manuscripts Data Portal', 'https://sinaiportal.library.ucla.edu/about', target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener noreferrer' %></strong> — An open-access, NEH-funded research data platform providing access to the manuscript descriptions and related authority files for St. Catherine’s Monastery in the Sinai, built on a data model designed to better represent the complexities inherent to early manuscripts.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          St. Catherine’s Monastery of the Sinai preserves one of the world’s oldest continually operating libraries. It holds some 4,559 manuscript codices, with works in 13 languages written from the 4th to the 19th century. These include the New Finds, a cache of approximately 1,100 fragmentary manuscripts discovered in 1975 when monks cleared debris from an earthquake-damaged room in the north wall of the Monastery.
        </p>
      </div>

      <hr class='divider'>

      <div class='static-page__row'>
        <h3 class='static-page__subtitle static-page__subtitle--sinai'>St. Catherine's Monastery and Its Library</h3>
          <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
            Located in Egypt’s Sinai Peninsula, the Orthodox “Sacred Monastery of the God-Trodden Mount Sinai,” commonly known as St. Catherine’s Monastery of the Sinai, is one of the world’s oldest monasteries and a UNESCO World Heritage Site. In the 6th century the Byzantine Emperor Justinian founded the Monastery and built it to approximately its current form, though monks had occupied the site by the 4th century. From the 4th century until today, the monks of Sinai have celebrated the Orthodox liturgy in unbroken continuity and have welcomed pilgrims from around the world, which accounts for the diversity of languages represented in its library.
          </p>
          <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
            Protected by its remote location on the Sinai Peninsula and its centuries-old bond with its Muslim neighbors, the Monastery preserves one of the world’s most important libraries. Its 4,559 manuscripts date from the 4th to the 19th century and are surprisingly diverse. Containing texts in 13 languages, they cover a range of genres, including books of the Bible, liturgy, hagiography, patristics, spirituality, and history, as well as a small but important number of classical poetic, scientific and philosophical texts. The Monastery’s collection of Greek manuscripts is considered to be second only to that of the Vatican, and the Monastery preserves arguably the most important collection of early Syriac and Christian Arabic manuscripts. Together the manuscripts of St. Catherine’s Monastery provide unparalleled evidence for the literature and history of Christians in the Eastern Mediterranean and shed light on the relations between Christian and Muslim communities.
          </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
